b) Prove that n! is in O(nⁿ)
We know:
n! = 1 × 2 × 3 × ... × n
Every number is less than or equal to n.
So:
n! ≤ n × n × n × ... × n
There are n terms, therefore:
n! ≤ nⁿ
Let:
c = 1
n₀ = 1
Therefore:
n! ∈ O(nⁿ)


c) Prove that 2n + 10 is in O(n)
For n ≥ 10:
10 ≤ n
Therefore:
2n + 10 ≤ 2n + n
2n + 10 ≤ 3n
Let:
c = 3
n₀ = 10
Therefore:
2n + 10 ∈ O(n)


d) Prove that 10 is in O(1)
